### What is RDS?

Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service) is a managed service that runs relational database engines. You choose an engine (PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB, Oracle, or SQL Server), an instance size, and storage, and AWS handles the heavy operational tasks. The core value is removing undifferentiated work so your team focuses on schema and queries instead of operations.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.rds.RdsClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.rds.model.CreateDbInstanceRequest;

public class CreateLedgerDatabase {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RdsClient rds = RdsClient.create();
        rds.createDBInstance(CreateDbInstanceRequest.builder()
                .dbInstanceIdentifier("nbi-ledger")
                .engine("postgres")
                .engineVersion("15.4")
                .dbInstanceClass("db.r6g.large")     // memory optimized for DB
                .allocatedStorage(100)                 // 100 GB gp3
                .masterUsername("nbi_admin")
                .masterUserPassword("initial-password") // rotate immediately
                .multiAZ(true)                         // synchronous standby
                .storageEncrypted(true)                // encryption at rest
                .build());
        System.out.println("RDS instance creation initiated");
    }
}
```

### How do automated backups work?

RDS takes daily snapshots and continuously captures transaction logs, giving point-in-time recovery so you can restore the database to any second within your retention window (up to 35 days). On EC2 you would script your own `pg_dump` or snapshot process, store and test them, and build your own point-in-time recovery, which is error-prone.

```bash
# Restore to a specific point in time (e.g., just before an accidental DELETE)
aws rds restore-db-instance-to-point-in-time \
  --source-db-instance-identifier nbi-ledger \
  --target-db-instance-identifier nbi-ledger-restored \
  --restore-time "2024-01-15T10:23:45Z"
# Creates a new instance restored to exactly that second
```

### How does patching work in RDS vs EC2?

RDS applies engine minor-version and operating-system patches during a maintenance window you choose, so security fixes land without you building a patch pipeline. On EC2 you track CVEs, test patches, and apply them yourself – miss one and you have a known vulnerability.

```text
# RDS maintenance window configuration
Maintenance window: Sun:02:00-Sun:03:00 (IST, low traffic)
Auto minor version upgrade: enabled
# AWS patches the engine and OS during this window
# Application reconnects automatically after brief restart

# On EC2 you would need:
# 1. Monitor CVE announcements
# 2. Test patch in staging
# 3. Schedule downtime window
# 4. Apply patch manually
# 5. Verify database starts correctly
# 6. Monitor for regressions
```

### How does Multi-AZ provide high availability?

With Multi-AZ, RDS keeps a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone. Every committed write goes to both the primary and the standby before it is acknowledged, so the standby is always current. If the primary fails, RDS automatically fails over to the standby, usually in one to two minutes, and the application reconnects to the same DNS endpoint.

```java
public class MultiAZFailover {
    // The application connects to ONE endpoint
    static final String DB_URL =
            "jdbc:postgresql://nbi-ledger.abcdef.ap-south-1.rds.amazonaws.com:5432/ledger";

    // After a failover, the same DNS endpoint points to the promoted standby
    // No code change, no config change needed
    // Connection pool detects the brief outage and reconnects automatically

    // On EC2 you would need:
    // 1. Set up streaming replication manually
    // 2. Build a failover script (or use Patroni/pg_auto_failover)
    // 3. Manage DNS updates
    // 4. Test failover regularly
}
```

### How do read replicas scale reads?

A read replica is an asynchronous copy you can send read-only queries to, offloading heavy reporting from the primary. You can have multiple replicas, in the same or other Regions. Replication lag is typically under a second.

```java
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.Connection;

public class ReadReplicaRouting {
    // Primary endpoint for writes
    static final String WRITE_URL =
            "jdbc:postgresql://nbi-ledger.abcdef.ap-south-1.rds.amazonaws.com:5432/ledger";

    // Read replica endpoint for reporting
    static final String READ_URL =
            "jdbc:postgresql://nbi-ledger-replica.abcdef.ap-south-1.rds.amazonaws.com:5432/ledger";

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Transfers write to primary
        Connection writeConn = DriverManager.getConnection(WRITE_URL, "transfer_app", "pass");

        // Month-end reports read from replica – no impact on live transfers
        Connection readConn = DriverManager.getConnection(READ_URL, "report_ro", "pass");
    }
}
```

### How is RDS different from a database on EC2?

| Aspect | RDS (Managed) | Database on EC2 (Self-Managed) |
|---|---|---|
| Backups | Automated daily snapshots + continuous logs; point-in-time recovery | You script pg_dump, test restores, manage retention |
| Patching | Automated in maintenance window | You track CVEs, test, and apply manually |
| High Availability | Multi-AZ with automatic failover (checkbox) | You set up replication + failover scripting |
| Read Scaling | Read replicas with a click | You configure streaming replication manually |
| OS Access | No shell access | Full root/admin access |
| Engine control | Supported versions and parameter groups only | Any version, any extension, any tuning |
| Cost | Instance + storage + backup retention | Instance + storage + your operations team time |

```java
public class RDSvsEC2Decision {
    // Use RDS when:
    // - Standard engine version is sufficient
    // - You want automated backups, patching, failover
    // - You want to minimize operational burden

    // Use EC2 when:
    // - You need OS-level access or custom kernel tuning
    // - You need an unsupported engine version or extension
    // - You need full control over replication topology
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is not enabling Multi-AZ for production databases. A single-AZ RDS instance means a hardware failure or AZ outage causes downtime. Multi-AZ adds cost but provides the automatic failover that banking requires.

The second mistake is using the master user for application connections. The master is for administration; create least-privilege users per service.

The third mistake is ignoring backup retention and restore testing. Having backups is useless if you have never tested restoring from them.

```bash
# Test a point-in-time restore quarterly
aws rds restore-db-instance-to-point-in-time \
  --source-db-instance-identifier nbi-ledger \
  --target-db-instance-identifier nbi-ledger-restore-test \
  --restore-time "2024-01-15T10:00:00Z"
# Verify data integrity on the restored instance
# Then delete the test instance
```

### What about Aurora?

Amazon Aurora pushes the managed model further with a distributed storage layer. Aurora separates compute from storage, replicates data six ways across three Availability Zones, and provides faster failover (typically under 30 seconds) than standard RDS Multi-AZ. It is compatible with PostgreSQL and MySQL.

For banking workloads that need the highest availability and performance, Aurora is often the best choice within the managed-database spectrum.

```text
# Aurora architecture
┌────────── Aurora Cluster ──────────┐
│                                     │
│  Writer Instance ──→ Shared         │
│  Reader Instance ──→ Distributed    │
│  Reader Instance ──→ Storage Layer  │
│                      (6 copies      │
│                       across 3 AZs) │
│                                     │
│  Failover: ~30 seconds              │
│  Standard RDS Multi-AZ: ~90 seconds │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

RDS trades some control for reliability. You cannot get OS shell access, you are limited to supported engine versions and parameters exposed through parameter groups, and very specialized tuning or unsupported extensions may force you onto EC2. For most banking workloads the reliability and reduced operational burden of RDS far outweigh that lost control.

A senior engineer also knows about RDS Proxy, which pools database connections to handle Lambda's connection-bursting pattern, and about cross-Region read replicas for disaster recovery. They right-size RDS instances using Performance Insights and CloudWatch, and they use encryption at rest with KMS and in-transit with SSL.

```bash
# Enable Performance Insights for query-level monitoring
aws rds modify-db-instance \
  --db-instance-identifier nbi-ledger \
  --enable-performance-insights \
  --performance-insights-retention-period 731  # 2 years for banking audit
```
